{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 42, :comments ["And it says <b>it is a night of watchings for HaShem, to bring them out from the land of Egypt</b> — because the matter was watched for them from the time He decreed the exile upon them, that He would bring them out on this night when the appointed time arrived immediately, as it says, 'At the time I will hasten it' (Isaiah 60:22). Or its meaning is, <b>it is a night of watchings for HaShem</b>, that He watches and looks forward to the night on which He will bring them out from the land of Egypt, for HaShem looks forward to the time when they will be worthy of being brought out from there.\n\nNow if we say that the meaning of 'for your seed shall be a stranger' is from the time that you have seed, and we begin the reckoning from the birth of Isaac, their stay in Egypt would be 240 years according to the explanation we mentioned. This too, in my opinion, is not the correct way of the plain sense, for all the days of Abraham, his seed cannot be called 'in exile.' The correct reading is that 'for your seed shall be a stranger in a land not theirs, four hundred years' should be counted from that very day. The meaning is to say to him: Your children will not inherit this land which I am giving them immediately, but they will be strangers like you in a land not theirs for four hundred years, and furthermore they shall not return here until the fourth generation, to complete four hundred and thirty. If so, their stay in Egypt would be approximately 210 years, or close to that. And if the calculation of the letters of 'רדו שמה' (Genesis 42:2) is a received tradition in Israel, it is possible that it alludes to those who themselves went down, for after Jacob's death they would remain there 210 years; and with 17, it would be 227.\n\nI have already mentioned (at Genesis 15:13) the explanation of Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra, ad loc.) who said that 'for your seed shall be a stranger' means in servitude and affliction for four hundred years from that very day, and he said that the additional thirty years were from the day Abraham left his land. If so, the meaning of the verse would be: 'and the sojourn of the children of Israel, who dwelt in Egypt, was thirty years and four hundred years' — for them and their fathers to have been strangers in a land not theirs.\n\nFurthermore, I say that the most manifest of all plain senses is that the decree was four hundred years from that day, as we mentioned, and these thirty years are an addition upon them because of the sin of that generation. For if a man is decreed, for his sin, one or two years of exile and affliction, and he adds further transgression upon his sin during them, they will add sevenfold upon his punishments — exile and suffering alike — for his first punishment is no guarantee to him that he will not be punished for the sin he may yet commit. Now upon Abraham our father there was a decree that his seed would be strangers in a land not theirs for four hundred years, and that they would not return here until the fourth generation, 'for the iniquity of the Amorite is not yet complete,' and he had no promise except 'and afterward they shall come out with great possessions,' and that immediately, or somewhat delayed, and even that was conditional, as He said, 'and also the nation whom they shall serve, I will judge, and afterward they shall come out with great possessions' — that He will judge the nation according to whether they acted fittingly toward Israel, in accordance with their deeds and in accordance with what was decreed upon them. Furthermore, there is no promise that sin will not cause it to be nullified except in the case of an oath.\n\nIt is well known that Israel in Egypt were very wicked and sinful, and they even neglected circumcision, as it is written (Ezekiel 20:8), 'But they rebelled against Me, and would not hearken to Me; they did not every man cast away the detestable things of their eyes, neither did they forsake the idols of Egypt,' and it is written (Joshua 24:14), 'and put away the gods which your fathers served beyond the River, and in Egypt, and serve HaShem.' Therefore their exile was lengthened by thirty years, and it was fitting that it be lengthened more, except that they cried out and multiplied prayer. This is the meaning of 'and they sighed from the bondage, and they cried out, and their cry rose up, and Elohim heard their groaning' (above 2:23–24); 'and now, behold, the cry of the children of Israel has come to Me' (ibid. 3:9); and it is written (Deuteronomy 26:7), 'and we cried to HaShem, the Elohim of our fathers, and HaShem heard our voice, and saw our affliction, and our labor, and our oppression' — for they were not worthy of being redeemed because of the appointed time that had arrived, but He accepted their cry and groaning because of the great distress they were in, as I explained in the section of Ve'elleh Shemot (2:25).\n\nWhy should it be difficult for the early commentators to explain that their exile was extended thirty years beyond the appointed end? Behold, it was extended upon them forty years because of the sin of the spies, for all those forty years they had affliction, as He said (Deuteronomy 8:2), 'and you shall remember all the way which HaShem your Elohim has led you these forty years in the wilderness, to afflict you'; and it is written (ibid. 3), 'and He afflicted you, and suffered you to hunger.' They had a complete exile in a land not theirs, with only fiery serpent and scorpion, and 'and the fourth generation shall return here' was not fulfilled for them, for in forty years that generation was surely replaced by others already born — but the sins caused everything. And it is possible that this was the cause of the children of Ephraim who went out thirty years before the entry of Moses our master, as our Sages mentioned (Sanhedrin 92a), for they counted correctly and did not err, but 'his own iniquities shall take the wicked' (Proverbs 5:22). And may HaShem forgive us for every sin and error." "The meaning of <b>it is this night for HaShem, a night of watchings for all the children of Israel throughout their generations</b> is that this night, which was watched for HaShem to bring them out of Egypt, is <b>for HaShem</b>, that is to say, consecrated to His name — <b>watchings for all the children of Israel throughout their generations</b>, that they shall keep it to serve before Him on it through the eating of the Paschal offering and the remembrance of the miracles, and to give praise and thanksgiving to His name, as He said (Exodus 13:10), 'and you shall keep this statute,' and He said (Deuteronomy 16:1), 'Observe the month of Aviv.' Rabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 12:42) said that the meaning of <b>it is a night of watchings for HaShem</b> is that HaShem watched over them and did not allow the destroyer to enter their houses. This is not correct, because it says <b>to bring them out from the land of Egypt</b>."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The verse citations 'above 2:23–24' and 'ibid. 3:9' refer to earlier passages in Exodus; Ramban writes simply לעיל (above) and שם (there), resolved by context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:17:11.330632501Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}